\title{Gauge-Invariant Obstructions to Periodic Impossible Figures}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{19 July 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
Periodic impossible figures can be modeled by assigning horizontal and vertical increments to the edges of a finite grid with opposite sides identified. Such a field is developable when the increments are differences of a single-valued height function. We give a self-contained classification over an arbitrary additive commutative coefficient group. The complete obstruction consists of local discrete curvature and two global periods around the fundamental cycles of the torus. A field is developable exactly when all three components vanish. Changing the local reference height adds a discrete gradient; curvature, both periods, and hence developability are invariant under every such gauge transformation. We give a constructive integration algorithm, linear-time decision procedures, explicit local and global counterexamples, and a uniformly descending periodic waterfall that remains impossible after a nonconstant checkerboard gauge change. We also distinguish periodic geometric staircases from the strictly descending filtration $2^k\mathbb Z$, whose intersection is zero but whose index set does not close into a cycle. The results identify the cohomological structure behind Escher-type staircases and clarify why claims about Penrose figures in manifolds require additional projection and depth-order semantics.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
Impossible staircases exploit a discrepancy between local and global perception. Every neighboring pair of steps can suggest a legitimate increase in height, while a complete circuit returns to the same visible location. A mathematical model should therefore identify local consistency, the global loops that must be checked, and the quantities that survive a change in the arbitrary reference used to label heights.

A periodic rectangular grid provides the simplest setting in which all three questions are nontrivial. Identifying opposite edges turns the grid into a discrete torus. Horizontal and vertical edge labels prescribe proposed increments in an additive commutative group $A$. If they arise from a vertex potential, or height function, the picture is developable. Otherwise no single-valued height realizes all prescribed changes.

On a simply connected rectangular patch, vanishing circulation around each elementary tile suffices for integrability. On a torus it does not. Two noncontractible cycles remain, winding horizontally and vertically. Their accumulated increments are periods. Periodic impossibility consequently has a local component, measured by curvature, and a global component, measured by two periods.

The principal result says that these are the only obstructions. A second result establishes gauge invariance: adding the gradient of any local reference function changes individual increments but neither curvature nor either period. The treatment is entirely additive and applies to integers, real numbers, vectors, finite cyclic groups, and arbitrary additive commutative groups.

\section{Periodic grids and increment fields}
Fix positive integers $m,n$ and write
\[
T_{m,n}=\mathbb Z/m\mathbb Z\times\mathbb Z/n\mathbb Z.
\]
Let $A$ be an additive commutative group. Indices are interpreted modulo $m$ and $n$.

\begin{definition}[Increment field]
An increment field is a pair $a,b:T_{m,n}\to A$. The value $a(i,j)$ labels the oriented edge from $(i,j)$ to $(i+1,j)$, while $b(i,j)$ labels the edge from $(i,j)$ to $(i,j+1)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Discrete derivatives]
For $g:T_{m,n}\to A$, define
\[
(\Delta_xg)(i,j)=g(i+1,j)-g(i,j),\qquad
(\Delta_yg)(i,j)=g(i,j+1)-g(i,j).
\]
The pair $(\Delta_xg,\Delta_yg)$ is the discrete gradient of $g$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Developability]
The field $(a,b)$ is developable if there exists $h:T_{m,n}\to A$ such that
\[
a=\Delta_xh,\qquad b=\Delta_yh.
\]
Thus every edge increment is the difference of one globally single-valued height at its endpoint and start point.
\end{definition}

\section{Local curvature and global periods}
\begin{definition}[Discrete curvature]
The curvature at the tile based at $(i,j)$ is
\[
C_{a,b}(i,j)=a(i,j)+b(i+1,j)-a(i,j+1)-b(i,j).
\]
It is the oriented sum around the tile boundary, equivalently $C_{a,b}=\Delta_xb-\Delta_ya$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Fundamental periods]
The horizontal and vertical periods are
\[
P_x(a)=\sum_{i=0}^{m-1}a(i,0),\qquad
P_y(b)=\sum_{j=0}^{n-1}b(0,j).
\]
They are the accumulated increments along representatives of the two fundamental cycles.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Row and column independence]
If $C_{a,b}=0$, then the sum of $a$ along a complete row is independent of the row, and the sum of $b$ along a complete column is independent of the column.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Sum $C_{a,b}(i,j)=0$ over $i$. The sum of $b(i+1,j)$ is a cyclic reindexing of the sum of $b(i,j)$, so these terms cancel. The remaining equality says that the horizontal sums of $a$ in rows $j$ and $j+1$ agree. Summing over $j$ instead proves the column statement.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Gradients are flat]
For every $h:T_{m,n}\to A$, one has $C_{\Delta_xh,\Delta_yh}=0$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Expand the four edge differences. Each vertex value occurs once positively and once negatively. This is the discrete equality of mixed derivatives.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Gradient periods vanish]
For every $h:T_{m,n}\to A$,
\[
P_x(\Delta_xh)=0,\qquad P_y(\Delta_yh)=0.
\]
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Each period is a cyclic telescoping sum. For example,
\[
\sum_{i=0}^{m-1}(h(i+1,0)-h(i,0))=0,
\]
because periodic identification makes the terminal point the initial point.
\end{proof}

\section{Complete classification}
\begin{theorem}[Periodic Developability Theorem]
Let $(a,b)$ be an increment field on $T_{m,n}$ with values in $A$. It is developable if and only if
\[
C_{a,b}=0,\qquad P_x(a)=0,\qquad P_y(b)=0.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
If $(a,b)$ is a gradient, the preceding two lemmas prove necessity.

Conversely, fix $(0,0)$ and give it height zero. For a vertex $v$, choose an edge path from $(0,0)$ to $v$ and define $h(v)$ as the signed sum of increments along that path. Reversing an edge contributes the negative of its forward label. To prove path independence, concatenate one path with the reverse of another. This makes a closed walk. Inserting or deleting an elementary tile boundary changes its sum by a curvature value and therefore by zero. After all contractible tile boundaries have been removed, a closed walk on the toroidal grid reduces to an integer combination of a horizontal and a vertical fundamental cycle. Its sum is consequently an integer combination of $P_x(a)$ and $P_y(b)$, hence zero. Thus $h$ is well defined. Appending one horizontal or vertical edge to a path proves respectively that $a=\Delta_xh$ and $b=\Delta_yh$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Complete obstruction triple]
Define
\[
\mathcal O(a,b)=(C_{a,b},P_x(a),P_y(b)).
\]
Then $(a,b)$ is developable exactly when $\mathcal O(a,b)=(0,0,0)$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{corollary}[Two modes of impossibility]
Every nondevelopable field has a witness consisting of either a tile of nonzero curvature, a nonzero horizontal period, or a nonzero vertical period. The first is local; the latter two are global and can occur even when every tile is consistent.
\end{corollary}

\section{Gauge transformations}
\begin{definition}[Gauge shift]
For $g:T_{m,n}\to A$, define the gauge-shifted field $(a^g,b^g)$ by
\[
a^g=a+\Delta_xg,
\qquad b^g=b+\Delta_yg.
\]
This changes local reference heights at the vertices.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Curvature invariance]
For every $(a,b)$ and $g$, one has $C_{a^g,b^g}=C_{a,b}$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Curvature is additive, so
\[
C_{a^g,b^g}=C_{a,b}+C_{\Delta_xg,\Delta_yg}.
\]
The second term vanishes because gradients are flat.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Period invariance]
For every $(a,b)$ and $g$,
\[
P_x(a^g)=P_x(a),\qquad P_y(b^g)=P_y(b).
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
For example,
\[
P_x(a^g)=P_x(a)+P_x(\Delta_xg)=P_x(a),
\]
and the vertical proof is identical.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Gauge-Invariant Classification]
For every gauge function $g$, the shifted field $(a^g,b^g)$ is developable if and only if $(a,b)$ is developable. Equivalently,
\[
(a^g,b^g)\text{ is developable}
\iff C_{a,b}=0,\quad P_x(a)=0,\quad
P_y(b)=0.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The preceding theorems give $\mathcal O(a^g,b^g)=\mathcal O(a,b)$. Apply the Periodic Developability Theorem. Directly, a potential $h$ for $(a,b)$ gives the potential $h+g$ for the shifted field, and subtraction of $g$ gives the converse.
\end{proof}

\section{Examples and boundary cases}
\begin{example}[Local defect]
Over $\mathbb Z$, set every increment to zero except one horizontal edge with value $1$. A tile incident to that edge has nonzero curvature. The field is nondevelopable regardless of its periods.
\end{example}

\begin{example}[Flat, globally impossible waterfall]
On a $3\times3$ torus over $\mathbb R$, let
\[
a(i,j)=-1,
\qquad b(i,j)=0.
\]
Every curvature equals $-1+0-(-1)-0=0$, but
\[
P_x(a)=-3,
\qquad P_y(b)=0.
\]
The field is locally consistent and globally impossible: three rightward steps return to the same vertex while demanding a drop of three units.

Take the nonconstant checker gauge $g(i,j)=i-j$, using standard representatives $i,j\in\{0,1,2\}$. Boundary jumps make the transformed labels nonuniform, but gauge invariance gives
\[
C_{a^g,b^g}=0,
\qquad P_x(a^g)=-3,
\qquad P_y(b^g)=0.
\]
No local reference change can develop this waterfall.
\end{example}

\begin{example}[Developable nonconstant field]
Choose any nonconstant periodic height $h$ and set $a=\Delta_xh$, $b=\Delta_yh$. The labels can be nonzero and can jump across representative boundaries, yet all curvatures and periods vanish. Integrating reconstructs $h$ up to an additive constant.
\end{example}

\begin{remark}[Boundary dimensions and coefficients]
The theorem remains valid when $m=1$ or $n=1$; loop edges make the corresponding period essential. The proof uses no order or norm. For $A=\mathbb Z/q\mathbb Z$ it tests consistency modulo $q$, and for $A=\mathbb R^d$ it tests vector displacements componentwise.
\end{remark}

\section{Algorithms}
\subsection{Obstruction computation and decision}
For every tile compute
\[
C(i,j)=a(i,j)+b(i+1,j)-a(i,j+1)-b(i,j),
\]
and compute $P_x$ and $P_y$ along the selected row and column. The field is developable exactly when all outputs vanish. The calculation uses $O(mn)$ group operations. Retaining the curvature table takes $O(mn)$ storage; a decision-only implementation can stop at the first nonzero tile and use constant auxiliary storage. The linear running time is optimal up to constants when every input label may need inspection.

\subsection{Potential reconstruction}
When the obstruction vanishes, set $h(0,0)=0$. Integrate horizontally along row zero by
\[
h(i+1,0)=h(i,0)+a(i,0),
\]
then vertically in each column by
\[
h(i,j+1)=h(i,j)+b(i,j).
\]
Verify the remaining horizontal and periodic closing edges. The classification theorem guarantees success in exact arithmetic. Reconstruction requires $O(mn)$ operations and $O(mn)$ output storage. Any two potentials differ by an additive constant because the grid is connected.

\subsection{Gauge audit}
Given $g$, form $(a^g,b^g)$ and compute the obstruction before and after shifting. Their equality is guaranteed and supplies a software diagnostic. Integer or rational arithmetic gives exact equality; floating-point calculations should use a scale-aware tolerance because telescoping cancellation can accumulate roundoff.

\section{A distinct notion of infinite staircase}
\begin{theorem}[Power-of-two filtration]
For $k\geq0$, let $I_k=2^k\mathbb Z$. Then
\[
I_0\supsetneq I_1\supsetneq I_2\supsetneq\cdots
\]
and
\[
\bigcap_{k=0}^{\infty}I_k=\{0\}.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Inclusion follows from $2^{k+1}\mathbb Z\subseteq2^k\mathbb Z$. It is strict because $2^k$ belongs to $I_k$ but not $I_{k+1}$. If $z$ belongs to every $I_k$, every power of two divides $z$. For nonzero $z$, choose $k$ with $2^k>|z|$; then divisibility is impossible. Hence $z=0$.
\end{proof}

This filtration has a beginning and continues indefinitely; it never identifies a later stage with an earlier one. A periodic geometric staircase instead lies on a closed cycle and is obstructed by nonzero additive holonomy. Infinite algebraic descent is therefore not a realization of periodic ascent.

\section{Cohomological interpretation}
Vertex functions are $0$-cochains, edge increments are $1$-cochains, and tile curvatures are $2$-cochains. The gradient is the coboundary from degree zero to degree one, while curvature is the next coboundary. Gradients are flat because $d^2=0$.

A zero-curvature field is closed, while a developable field is exact. Closed fields on a torus need not be exact because first cohomology is nontrivial. Horizontal and vertical periods evaluate a closed field on generators of the two independent cycle directions; their vanishing characterizes exactness. Gauge transformations add exact $1$-cochains, leaving closed-loop periods unchanged. The obstruction triple thus packages both the local coboundary and the global cohomology class.

This viewpoint predicts an extension to finite cell complexes: test curvature on all $2$-cells and periods on generators of first homology. The torus is special only in having two canonical global generators.

\section{Applications}
\paragraph{Phase unwrapping.} Sensors often observe phase modulo a fixed period. Neighboring phase differences form edge increments. Nonzero tile curvature signals a local residue, while global periods detect inconsistency caused by periodic boundary conditions. Gauge shifts change local representatives without changing these obstructions.

\paragraph{Depth integration.} Estimated depth gradients in computer vision must be integrated to recover a surface. Local curl tests detect incompatible neighboring estimates. Periodic textures and panoramas introduce global cycle constraints that local tests miss.

\paragraph{Robotic loop closure.} Relative displacement measurements should sum to zero around closed loops. Changing the selected origin is a gauge operation and cannot alter loop error. The toroidal grid is a structured case where a small generating family of cycles gives a complete test.

\paragraph{Periodic design.} A repeated relief pattern may prescribe edge-to-edge height differences. Seamless fabrication requires zero local curvature and zero rise across each periodic direction before metric embedding constraints are considered.

\section{Scope and geometric semantics}
The additive classification does not imply that every non-orientable three-manifold contains a Penrose triangle. Such a claim requires a definition involving projection, depth order, beam geometry, visibility, and a clear distinction between embeddings and immersions. Non-orientability concerns reversal of orientation under transport; a Penrose triangle is an optical contradiction produced by projection-dependent depth relations. A future theory may connect the two through twisted coefficients or orientation covers, but topology alone does not provide the needed optical data.

Developability here means integrability of increments, not automatically realization as a bendable sheet in Euclidean three-space. Metric realization adds edge lengths, face shapes, and nonintersection conditions. The additive theorem is a foundational compatibility test rather than a complete metric embedding theorem.

\section{Discussion and future work}
The classification separates local and global consistency. Curvature may vanish while holonomy remains nonzero, as in the waterfall. Gauge transformations may alter every displayed edge while preserving both kinds of inconsistency. This explains why an impossible staircase can be locally flawless and why relabeling cannot repair it.

For an arbitrary connected finite two-dimensional cell complex, one expects developability exactly when cellular curvature vanishes and periods vanish on a generating family of cycles. A related algorithmic problem seeks minimal certificates: either one defective tile or a short simple noncontractible cycle with nonzero period.

A rigorous Penrose-beam model should specify a projection and depth-order relation. Its obstruction may be a cyclic depth-order cocycle whose acyclicity after subdivision characterizes realizability. For non-orientable spaces, increments twisted by the orientation local system suggest testing periods on the orientation double cover together with anti-invariance under the deck transformation.

Finally, metric realization asks whether a flat, period-free increment field admits a piecewise-linear developable surface with prescribed edge lengths. Additive integrability is necessary, but every tile must also satisfy geometric compatibility conditions.

\section{Conclusion}
A periodic impossible figure has a complete additive obstruction. Tile curvature records local failure; horizontal and vertical periods record global failure around the fundamental directions of the torus. Their simultaneous vanishing is equivalent to a single-valued height function.

Changing local references adds a gradient. Mixed differences cancel around tiles and gradient sums telescope around periodic cycles. Curvature, periods, the full obstruction triple, and developability are consequently gauge invariant.

The same theory distinguishes a genuinely periodic Escher staircase from the nonperiodic algebraic descent $2^k\mathbb Z$. More broadly, it illustrates a general rule: local increments acquire global meaning only after every contractible and noncontractible loop has been accounted for.

