## Infinite descent is not periodic ascent

The ideals $2^k\mathbb Z$ form a genuine infinite descending chain, but no stage is identified with an earlier stage.

{{interactive_demo:2}}

{{visualization:2}}

<details><summary>Reveal the zero-intersection argument</summary>

The inclusion $2^{k+1}\mathbb Z\subsetneq2^k\mathbb Z$ is strict because $2^k$ is absent from the next ideal. If a nonzero integer belonged to every level, it would be divisible by powers of two larger than its absolute value, which is impossible. Hence the intersection is $\{0\}$.

</details>

